#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""imap-sync-tests -- the milestone 3b imapsync module's acceptance
suite (src/ams/msclients/imapsync/imap_sync.c), the one-way
IMAP-to-local-store mirror. See revival/doc/claude-history/imap-sync-prompt.md.

Usage:
    revival/tests/imap-sync-tests

Live against the IMAP account named by AUIS_TEST_IMAP_HOST via the
committed netrc_Lookup and ~/.netrc (machine = that host), exactly as
imap-protocol-tests and smtp-protocol-tests do.
Strictly read-only against the mailbox from imapsync's own side: it only
ever issues EXAMINE/UID SEARCH/UID FETCH (FLAGS INTERNALDATE ENVELOPE)/
UID FETCH BODY.PEEK[] -- no STORE/APPEND/CREATE/DELETE/EXPUNGE reaches
the wire from imapsync. Case 3 sends one real message via the committed
SMTP path (smtptest.test) to the AUIS_TEST_ADDRESS mailbox only, as
the spec prescribes for the incremental-sync case.

All six cases run against one fresh `mktemp -d` scratch mspath root
(cases 3-6 build on the mirror cases 1-2 already produced) -- the real
`~/.IMAP/fastmail` is never touched, and the real `~/preferences` is
never touched (case 6 uses its own scratch PROFILES file). The scratch
root is removed on exit unless the environment variable
IMAPSYNC_KEEP_SCRATCH is set (useful for inspecting a failure by hand).

Case 1 mirrors the *entire* real INBOX (several thousand messages as of
this writing) and case 5's UIDVALIDITY drill re-mirrors it again from
scratch, so this suite can take a long time (tens of minutes) to run
live.

Configuration (no defaults; every case is skipped, with one message
listing what is missing, unless all four are set to non-blank values;
with any unset this suite makes no network connection at all):

    AUIS_TEST_ADDRESS    your own test mailbox address: the only
                         recipient of case 3's real send and the From:/To:
                         of every message appended or drafted here
    AUIS_TEST_IMAP_HOST  the IMAP server (~/.netrc machine = this host)
    AUIS_TEST_SMTP_HOST  the SMTP server for case 3's send (smtphost in a
                         scratch PROFILES; ~/.netrc machine = this host,
                         smtpsub.c:377)
    AUIS_TEST_FOLDER     the IMAP folder the W-cases write to. It MUST be
                         a dedicated scratch folder holding no real mail.

Limits from C code this suite does not change, reported as skip
reasons: imapsync connects only to imap.fastmail.com
(src/ams/msclients/imapsync/imap_sync.c:214-216, no override), so every
case here needs AUIS_TEST_IMAP_HOST=imap.fastmail.com; and imaptest.test's
write subcommands accept only "Revival/WritebackTest"
(src/overhead/mail/lib/imaptest.c:400), so the W-cases need
AUIS_TEST_FOLDER=Revival/WritebackTest.

The W-cases never empty a folder: before any write, preflight_folder()
lists AUIS_TEST_FOLDER (a read-only imapsync mirror into a throwaway
root) and refuses to run the W-cases, touching nothing, if any message's
Subject lacks one of HARNESS_SUBJECT_MARKERS; harness leftovers from
earlier runs are removed by exact UID. Cleanup removes only
marker-subject messages (plus UIDs this run's own APPENDs reported) by
exact UID set (UID STORE \\Deleted + UID EXPUNGE) -- never a range.

Cases W2/W3/W4/W7 (added for the writeback milestone's Gate 2) exercise
imap_sync.c's replay_folder() -- flags replay, purge replay, the
25-purge safety valve, and the stale-UIDVALIDITY skip -- against their
own fresh scratch root and mailbox, entirely separate from cases 1-6's
INBOX mirror above (own tempdir, own imapsync run, no dependency on the
long INBOX pass having completed). ALL destructive traffic in these
cases -- STORE/EXPUNGE/APPEND/CREATE, and every imapsync invocation
that might replay a journal -- is confined to one dedicated mailbox,
WRITE_MAILBOX (AUIS_TEST_FOLDER), created if absent; INBOX is never
written to anywhere in this suite. This is enforced twice,
independently: imaptest.c's own guard_mailbox() refuses at the C level
for the create/storeflags/expunge/append subcommands regardless of
caller, and this file's own run_imapsync_wb()/imaptest_wb() wrappers
refuse, themselves, to be called with any other mailbox/folder name --
a safety rule this important should not have exactly one enforcement
point. These four cases fabricate well-formed journal lines directly
(following msjournal.c's documented "J1 ..." grammar exactly) rather
than driving a real cui session to produce them: Gate 1's own
imap-writeback-tests suite already proves real capture end-to-end (its
case 1), so these cases test replay_folder() in isolation, faster and
more deterministically -- exactly the same testing strategy the
milestone's own spec calls for in case 4 ("fabricate a journal with 26
purges").

Cases W5/W6 (added for the writeback milestone's Gate 3-- note the
numbering gap left by W2/W3/W4/W7 above, evidently reserved for
exactly these) exercise append replay and its crash-resume contract.
Unlike W2/W3/W4/W7, replay_append_line() needs a real local native-id
body file plus a real .MS_MsgDir entry (via MS_GetSnapshot) to act on
-- there is no safe way to fabricate that from Python without
hand-writing .MS_MsgDir binary bytes, so both cases use
create_local_draft_via_cui() (a real cui "mail" -> compose -> "draft"
session -- MS_AppendFileToFolder, no IMAP, no SMTP) for the one real
fixture message each needs; W6 then fabricates the surrounding
journal/state-file conditions (a flags line that is deliberately never
applied, a renamed .MS_Journal.replaying, a manually pre-applied
"crashed run's own APPEND", and a journal_offset cursor set to point
exactly at the interrupted line) the same way W4 fabricates its own
edge case, to prove the crash-resume contract deterministically rather
than by timing a real process kill.
"""

